P9. rationalisability as a decision procedure
exact rational arithmetic throughout, no sampling and no tolerance

check one: the decider against P1's direction sweep
  regions 6, arms 5, sections 15625
  AN argmin, ties allowed   (this decider) : 72
  THE argmin, ties broken   (P1's sweep)   : 9

  the two differ, and the difference is exactly the tie degeneracy.
  arms tied on the residency axis          : packed, packed-simd
  mixtures of those across 6 regions       : 2^6 = 64
  strict sections, recomputed here         : 9
  in both sets                             : 1
  union                                    : 72
  decider's count                          : 72
  the union IS the decider's set           : True

  So the honest figure for the claim in section 2.4 is the weak one: 72 of
  15625, or 0.461%, because at a tie the design really may take either arm.
  Both figures are three orders below the section count.

check two: three cost dimensions, two cases with a known answer
  A: a dominates everywhere, section (a, a)      -> True  (expected True)
  A: a dominates everywhere, section (b, b)      -> False  (expected False)
  B: identical regions, section (a, a)           -> True  (expected True)
  B: identical regions, section (a, b)           -> True  (expected True)
     I expected False here and the decider was right. All three arms share
     the third coordinate, so the weighting (0, 0, 1) makes every arm tie,
     and under a tie either pick is an argmin. The case is kept with the
     correction rather than deleted.
  C: identical regions, no shared coordinate, section (a, b)
                                                 -> False  (expected False)

  A section that picks differently in two identical regions, where nothing
  ties the arms, cannot be an argmin of one weighting, and the decider says
  so. That is the shape of every inconsistency the check exists to catch,
  and case B is the reminder that a tie is not an inconsistency.

==================================================================
decider's weak count on the committed data, decomposed exactly   : 72
decider answers every constructed case correctly                : True

So the constraint section 2.4 proposes is not merely stateable, it is
decidable, in the number of cost dimensions the design actually uses, by
a procedure that runs once where a table is written and never at compile
time or at run time. What it costs to keep is one offline check.
